Regent Street is London's ultra luxury  shopping Boulevard more than a million  people stroll by its shop windows every  week but right now there's something  extraordinary to be seen there these are  not window displays their sculptures  designed by architects the Regent Street  windows project is trying to bring  architecture and consumerism together  it's attracted creative people from all  over Europe like tempest Laura kia from  Switzerland who works for Puma I think  they starting to come up with something  much more sculptural an autumn redesign  it comes up with something that's  probably an eye-catching in a different  way much more about harrowing product  certainly what I see from all of these  and especially this one here is it much  more about the sculpture much more about  the overall form a couple of meters  further on is the atrium of the fashion  label as free these three women are  fascinated by the wooden waves the women  work for a Swedish fashion designer and  are hoping to get some inspiration I  like that you walk into it and you have  like you can nurse  eat on above you when you feel like  having an amelia is deafening to all the  different textures like sand and would  then yeah summer feeling that summer  feeling is exactly what Debra Nagin  wanted to evoke with her installation  more than 80 architects applied for the  shop window project only six were chosen  Deborah Nagin was awarded the contract  for the Esprit atrium it soon became  clear that she wanted to tell a story  the origin of the Esprit store came in  the late 60s when the founders were  traveling in California and hanging out  on the beach and they wanted a lifestyle  store that was really relaxed as relaxed  as that as the life they were living and  so we thought that if we could recreate  the beach in the shop it would give  people the sense of being at the seaside  and being in California and tell the  background story to spray it was an  appealing challenge for the London  architect who usually works on quite  different projects we went for scale we  knew that within the space they offered  us we could make something that went  from the ground floor of the shop right  the way up to the ceiling and and onto  the first floor the idea wasn't easy to  implement its closing time before the  start of the project the installation  has to be done before the shop opens for  business the next day the whole thing  will have to be put in place in one  night that's not usually in the job  description for architects  it's not what we normally do okay and we  couldn't really practice it either so  what we're doing now experimental they  will work over as regent street shuts  down for the night things are frantic  behind the shop windows being used for  the projects Antonia Faust is a member  of the famous Royal Institute of British  Architects she's come up with a fresh  idea normally with retail design you  might have a quite as traditional  mannequin with a backdrop and what  they're doing here is creating a new  space a new kind of reinvention of the  space that all exists it's a the brand's  to kind of refresh themselves and be  seen in a new light it also kind of  invigorates their own design teams and  allows them to kind of approach project  in a totally new way as well meanwhile  back at a spree it's 2 a.m. and there is  a problem Debra's calculations were too  tight the biggest wave doesn't fit in  the room her team manages to find a  solution but not everyone might think it  looks like a beach  it's okay and I'm not tired but I wish I  wish we've been able to it faster but  it's not possible to do this fast work  will go on for the next few hours at  least it's now 10 a.m. and time to open  for business the finishing touches are  put to the beach Debra Nagin can't wait  to find out what people think of her  installation  then actually very successful so far  we've got people coming in people that  wouldn't usually come in and just you  know have a look it does bring something  else to the high street it brings a  little bit of culture to the high street  rather than just shopping shopping  shopping culture and commerce a winning  combination
 

